ch that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly two of the three lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nHence one of the three lines is **non\u2011sunny**; it is therefore either  \n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);  \n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);  \n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=d\\).\n\nWe examine these three possibilities.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The non\u2011sunny line is horizontal\n\nA horizontal line can contain at most the three points of \\(T_3\\) that share the same \\(y\\)-coordinate.  \nThe only choice that covers three points is \\(y=1\\), which contains  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,1),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any two points of \\(U\\) the slope is respectively  \n\n* \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\): \\(0\\) (horizontal),  \n* \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\): undefined (vertical),  \n* \\((2,2)-(1,3)\\): \\(-1\\).\n\nAll three slopes are prohibited for a sunny line. Consequently a sunny line can meet **at most one** point of \\(U\\). With only two sunny lines left we could cover at most two points of \\(U\\), leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nIf the horizontal line were \\(y=2\\) (covering \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)) or \\(y=3\\) (covering \\((1,3)\\)), it would leave at least four points uncovered. Any line through any two of those points again has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus the two sunny lines could cover at most two of the remaining points, which is insufficient. Hence a horizontal non\u2011sunny line makes the required configuration impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The non\u2011sunny line is vertical\n\nThe argument is symmetric to the horizontal case.  \nChoosing \\(x=1\\) (the only vertical line covering three points) leaves  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\},\n\\]\n\nand the slopes of the three possible pairs are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), none of which are sunny. Therefore the two sunny lines can cover at most two of the three points, leaving one uncovered.  \n\nIf the vertical line is \\(x=2\\) or \\(x=3\\) it covers at most two points, leaving at least four uncovered; the same slope analysis shows that any line through two of those points is non\u2011sunny, so the configuration is again impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. The non\u2011sunny line is diagonal \\(x+y=d\\)\n\nA line of the form \\(x+y=d\\) can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\).  \nThe admissible values of \\(d\\) are \\(2,3,4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1.  \\(d=2\\) (the line \\(x+y=2\\))\n\nThe diagonal covers only \\((1,1)\\).  \nThe remaining five points are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* No three points of \\(U\\) are collinear on a sunny line.\n\n*Proof of claim.*  \nWe examine all \\(\\binom{5}{3}=10\\) triples.  \nThe only collinear triple is \\((1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\), which lies on the line of slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny). Every other triple has at least two distinct slopes among its three sides, hence is not collinear. \u220e\n\nBecause any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(U\\), the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points of \\(U\\). Since \\(|U|=5\\), at least one point of \\(U\\) remains uncovered, contradicting the requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2.  \\(d=3\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\))\n\nThe diagonal covers \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(1,1),(3,1),(2,2),(1,3)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the six possible pairs are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1 \\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\infty \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(2,2)-(1,3) & -1 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus the **only** sunny line that can pass through two points of \\(U\\) is the line \\(y=x\\) (through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\)).  \nIf we use this line as one of the sunny lines, the two points \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) remain uncovered; any line through both has slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny). Hence the second sunny line cannot cover them simultaneously, and the configuration fails.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3.  \\(d=4\\) (the line \\(x+y=4\\))\n\nThe diagonal covers \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \nThe uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the six possible pairs are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1 \\ (\\text{sunny})\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\infty \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(2,2)-(1,2) & 0 \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\\\\n(2,2)-(2,1) & \\infty \\ (\\text{non\u2011sunny})\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAgain the only sunny line that can contain two points of \\(U\\) is \\(y=x\\) (through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\)).  \nAfter using this line, the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) remain; any line through them has slope \\(-1\\) (non\u2011sunny). Hence the second sunny line cannot cover both, and the configuration is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice for the unique non\u2011sunny line leads to a contradiction, **no arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(T_3\\)**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
