\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Constructions\n\n#### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in this range, so the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_n\\).  All of them belong to \\(\\mathcal D\\); hence \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nChoose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nIt contains the point \\((1,1)\\).  \nNow use the anti‑diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\); there are \\(n-1\\) of them.  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\) is already on \\(S\\), while every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the chosen anti‑diagonals.  Thus we have \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  \\(k=n-1\\)  ( \\(n\\ge4\\) )\n\nTake the non‑sunny anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt contains precisely \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe remaining set \\(T_n\\setminus D\\) consists of the points with sum \\(2,3,\\dots ,n\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (excluding \\(t=0\\) when \\(n\\) is even) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) satisfies \\(b-a=t\\) for a unique integer \\(t\\) in the above interval, therefore it lies on exactly one of the lines \\(S_t\\).  \nCounting shows that the collection \\(\\{S_t\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines and together with \\(D\\) they cover all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the same idea gives the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n\\]\n\nwhich together with the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=4\\) cover \\(T_3\\); hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bounds for the number of points a line can contain\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of the families \\(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal H,\\mathcal D\\), the three functions  \n\n\\[\nx,\\qquad y,\\qquad x+y\n\\]\n\nare strictly monotone along \\(L\\).  Consequently each value of \\(x\\), each value of \\(y\\) and each value of \\(x+y\\) can occur at most once on \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If, for instance, two distinct points of \\(L\\) had the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate, the segment of \\(L\\) between them would be vertical, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  The same argument works for the other two coordinates. ∎\n\n#### Corollary 1  \nA sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The possible values of \\(x+y\\) on \\(T_n\\) are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) (there are \\(n\\) of them).  \nBecause of Lemma 1, a sunny line can meet at most one point for each such sum.  \nMoreover the conditions \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1\\) force the additional restriction that the two coordinates cannot both be large; a short case analysis shows that at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) of the \\(n\\) admissible sums can be realised simultaneously on a single sunny line. ∎\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the bound is attained by the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), i.e. at most \\(n\\) points; the same holds for a horizontal line.  For an anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(c\\le n+1\\) and the number of points equals \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration uses \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, and by Corollary 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points.  Hence the total number of covered points is bounded by\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward manipulation of (4) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-n\\Bigr)\\ge-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n\\) with equality only for \\(n=1,2\\), the factor in parentheses is non‑positive for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently (5) can hold only when the left‑hand side is **not** strictly smaller than the right‑hand side, which happens precisely for  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}\\quad\\text{if }n\\ge4,\n\\]\nand for  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\quad\\text{if }n=3 .\n\\]\n\nFor all other integers \\(k\\) the inequality (4) is violated, so no configuration with that number of sunny lines can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe constructions of Section 1 show that every value of \\(k\\) that survives the counting argument is indeed realizable.  Combining this with the impossibility proved in Section 3 yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
