---------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there is a family\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\ge2\\) of them are sunny.  Let the sunny lines be\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and the non‑sunny ones be \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_{n-k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  A useful observation  \n\nA line (sunny or not) can meet a fixed column \\(C_a\\) in **at most one**\npoint, because two distinct points of the same column have the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate and any line containing both would be vertical, which is\nnon‑sunny; a non‑vertical line meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in a single\npoint.  Consequently, for every column \\(C_a\\)\n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(C_a\\cap\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\bigr)\\le |\\mathcal L| = n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Counting points in the right‑most \\(k\\) columns  \n\nConsider the right‑most \\(k\\) columns\n\\(C_{n-k+1},\\dots ,C_n\\).  By (1) they contain \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\nEach sunny line can contribute at most one point to each of these columns,\nhence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together contribute at most \\(k\\cdot k=k^{2}\\) points.\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can also contribute at most one point\nto each column, giving at most \\((n-k)k\\) further points.  Altogether the\n\\(n\\) lines can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk^{2}+(n-k)k = nk \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of the right‑most \\(k\\) columns.\n\nBecause all points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nnk\\ge \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n2n\\ge k+1 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(k\\ge2\\) and any \\(n\\ge3\\) inequality (4) is satisfied, so (3) does **not**\nby itself give a contradiction.  We therefore use a finer argument that\nexploits the *shape* of the