 of \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\nHence the only possible restriction is the obvious one \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSplit the set \\(T_n\\) into\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-k\\},\\\\[2mm]\nB &=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Covering \\(A\\).*  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-k\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(A\\) satisfy \\(x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), therefore they lie on\none of the lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\).  \nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Covering \\(B\\).*  \nWrite a point of \\(B\\) as \\((a,b)=(n-k+j,b)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le k+1-j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-x = -\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)+i .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently they are sunny and they are\npairwise distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in B\\) and set \\(j=a-(n-k)\\) (so \\(1\\le j\\le k\\)).\nChoose  \n\n\\[\ni:=b+j-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFrom (8) we obtain \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), hence \\(i\\) is a legitimate index.\nNow compute using (9):\n\n\\[\ny-x = -\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)+i\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nb-(n-k+j)= -\\bigl(n-k\\bigr)+(b+j-1)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nb = b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); i.e. it lies on the line\n\\(L_i\\).  Consequently every point of \\(B\\) belongs to one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The whole family of lines\n\nCollect the \\(n\\) distinct lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_k=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*   Exactly \\(k\\) of them (\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\)) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they\n    are sunny.\n*   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are vertical and hence non‑sunny.\n*   By the arguments of Sections 2.1 and 2.2 every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\n    least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\nboth requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and the\npreceding counting argument shows that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\nHence the set of admissible values is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
