            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non‑sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x=a\\), i.e. it lies on \\(V_a\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Corollary 1.**  \nThe value \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nFix any sunny line \\(S\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  \nThe family consisting of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_n\\) together with \\(S\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 the vertical lines \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_n\\) already cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is at least 2. The remaining points are those with \\(x=1\\); they are also covered by the vertical line \\(V_1\\) which we have omitted, but they are nevertheless covered by the sunny line \\(S\\) because \\(S\\) passes through infinitely many points, and we may choose \\(S\\) so that it contains at least one point with \\(x=1\\) (e.g. \\(y=x\\) contains \\((1,1)\\)). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines. Exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Corollary 2.**  \nThe value \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Remark on the previously claimed construction for \\(k=n\\).**  \nThe lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-(n+1)+i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nall have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.  \nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\) does **not** satisfy any of the equations (3) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y = 1-(n+1)+i = i-n\\), which equals \\(1\\) only when \\(i=n+1\\), a value outside the allowed range \\(1\\le i\\le n\\). Hence \\((1,1)\\) is uncovered, and the family (3) does **not** satisfy the covering requirement. Consequently the claim that \\(k=n\\) is always possible is unfounded.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Open Cases.**  \nThe arguments above settle the existence of configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \nNo construction is known to me for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny), and I have not succeeded in proving that configurations with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) cannot exist. Determining the full set of admissible \\(k\\) therefore remains an open problem. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps y