ey have\ndifferent slopes.  Adding them does not affect the covering already\nestablished, because a point that is already on a non\u2011sunny line remains on\nat least one line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Verification of the required properties\n\n*Number of lines.*  We have \\((n-k)\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the \\(V_i\\) and\n\\(H_j\\)) and \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)); altogether \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  By Sections\u202f2\u20133 every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of\nthe non\u2011sunny lines; the sunny lines may intersect the same points but do\nnot remove the covering.  Hence every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\nbelongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nA sunny line is a line, therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed\nthe total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).  Moreover the number of sunny\nlines is a non\u2011negative integer.  Consequently no integer outside the range\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can satisfy the conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above provides\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and covering all\npoints \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  No other integer \\(k\\)\nis possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"

