\\(T_{m+1}\\) with \\(a+b=m+2\\).  Removing those points leaves the\nset \\(T_m\\).  By the induction hypothesis there exist \\(m\\) sunny lines\n\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_m\\) covering \\(T_m\\).\n\nNow replace the non\u2011sunny line \\(D\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=(m+1)x-(m+1)(m)+ (m+2) .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) passes through the point \\((1,m+1)\\) and has slope\n\\(m+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it is sunny.  Moreover \\(L\\) also\ncontains the point \\((m+1,1)\\), i.e. it covers the whole set of points of\n\\(T_{m+1}\\) that were on \\(D\\).  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_m,L\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of\n\\(T_{m+1}\\).  This completes the induction. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\;( \\ge 2)\\).\n\n**Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,m-1)\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x\\le m-1\\) lies on one of the \\(V_j\\); every\npoint with \\(y=1\\) lies on \\(H\\).  The only points that may remain uncovered\nare those satisfying  \n\n\\[\nx\\ge m,\\qquad y\\ge 2,\\qquad x+y\\le n+1 . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIntroduce new coordinates  \n\n\\[\nx'=x-m+1,\\qquad y'=y-1 .\n\\]\n\nCondition (3.1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nx',y'\\ge 1,\\qquad x'+y'\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(T_k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nDenote these lines by \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n**Complete family.**  \nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{m-1},H,S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\( (m-1)+1+k = n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to 