            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence\n\\(k=0\\) is always possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – covering \\(T_m\\) with \\(m\\) sunny lines for every \\(m\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  \nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -2x+5,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac52 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_1\\\\\n(2,2) & L_1\\\\\n(1,2) & L_3\\\\\n(3,1) & L_3\\\\\n(1,3) & L_2\\\\\n(2,1) & L_2\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nso the three sunny lines cover \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge 3\\).  Consider \\(T_{m+1}\\).\nThe line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=m+2\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **non‑sunny**; it contains precisely the\npoints of \\(T_{m+1}\\) with \\(a+b=m+2\\).  Removing those points leaves the\nset \\(T_m\\).  By the induction hypothesis there exist \\(m\\) sunny lines\n\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_m\\) covering \\(T_m\\).\n\nNow replace the non‑sunny line \\(D\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=(m+1)x-(m+1)(m)+ (m+2) .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) passes through the point \\((1,m+1)\\) and has slope\n\\(m+1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it is sunny.  Moreover \\(L\\) also\ncontains the point \\((m+1,1)\\), i.e. it covers the whole set of points of\n\\(T_{m+1}\\) that were on \\(D\\).  Hence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_m,L\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of\n\\(T_{m+1}\\).  This completes the induction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\;( \\ge 2)\\).\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,m-1)\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x\\le m-1\\) lies on one of the \\(V_j\\); every\npoint with \\(y=1\\) lies on \\(H\\).  The only points that may remain uncovered\nare those satisfying  \n\n\\[\nx\\ge m,\\qquad y\\ge 2,\\qquad x+y\\le n+1 . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIntroduce new coordinates  \n\n\\[\nx'=x-m+1,\\qquad y'=y-1 .\n\\]\n\nCondition (3.1) becomes  \n\n\\[\nx',y'\\ge 1,\\qquad x'+y'\\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered set is exactly \\(T_k\\).\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nBy Lemma 1 the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nDenote these lines by \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n**Complete family.**  \nThe collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{m-1},H,S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\( (m-1)+1+k = n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence a configuration exists for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\n#### 4.1  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists.\nA non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*If it is vertical* \\(x=c\\), then it contains at most three points of\n\\(T_3\\); the two remaining points lie in different rows and different\ncolumns, so any sunny line can meet at most one of them (a sunny line is\nneither horizontal nor vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence the two\nremaining points cannot be covered by a single sunny line – contradiction.\n\nThe same argument works for a horizontal or a \\(-1\\)‑slope line.\nTherefore for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n#### 4.2  The general case \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nTake the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\nas the unique non‑sunny line (its slope is \\(-1\\)).  The points of \\(T_n\\)\nthat are not on \\(D\\) form the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\).  By Lemma 1,\n\\(T_{n-1}\\) can be covered by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Adding the line \\(D\\)\ngives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines,\ncovering all of \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every\n\\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nLemma 1 applied with \\(m=n\\) provides directly a covering of \\(T_n\\) by\n\\(n\\) sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  (For \\(n=3\\) the explicit three\nlines given in the base of Lemma 1 are used; for \\(n\\ge 4\\) the inductive\nconstruction of Lemma 1 yields the required family.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,3\\).\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 4\\) **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n  are admissible.\n\nThus the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
