            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nIf a line contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\) then the vector differences\nbetween any two of them have integer coordinates whose greatest common divisor\nis at least \\(2\\).  The only primitive direction vectors with this property in\nthe triangle are \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\), which correspond exactly to the three\nforbidden slopes.  Hence a sunny line can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Maximal coverage by non‑sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or diagonal\n(\\(x+y=c\\)).  For a fixed integer \\(d\\) the largest number of distinct points\nthat can be covered by \\(d\\) non‑sunny lines is obtained by taking the \\(d\\)\nvertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=d\\); they are pairwise disjoint and contain  \n\n\\[\nC(d)=\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(n+1-i)=\\frac{d\\,(2n-d+1)}2\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  No other choice of \\(d\\) non‑sunny lines can cover more,\nbecause any horizontal or diagonal line replaces a vertical line of the same\nor larger length by a line that contains at most the same number of points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\nThe remaining \\(d=n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, so by (1) they cover at most\n\\(C(d)\\) points.  Using the bound of §1 for the sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\nC(d)+2k\\ge |T_n|\n\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives \\(k(3-k)\\ge0\\).  Hence the\ninequality fails for every \\(k\\ge4\\); consequently **no configuration with\n\\(k\\ge4\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nThe two sunny lines can cover at most four points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the\nother \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-2,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-2,2),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAny line joining two of them is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\);\nhence none of the three lines can be sunny.  Consequently each of the points\nin (2) must be covered by a non‑sunny line.\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most two of the points in (2):\n* a vertical line \\(x=n-2\\) contains \\(P_1,P_2\\);\n* a vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) contains \\(P_3\\);\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\) contains \\(P_2,P_3\\);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(P_1,P_3\\).\n\nThus at least **two** of the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are forced to be\n\\(x=n-2\\) and \\(x=n-1\\) (or an equivalent pair).  After those two lines are\nused, every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x\\ge n-2\\) is already covered **except**\nthe three points listed in (2).  The two sunny lines can together cover at most\nfour points, but the three points of (2) are pairwise non‑sunny, so at most one\nof them can be covered by a sunny line.  Hence at least one point of (2) stays\nuncovered – a contradiction.\n\nTherefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n#### 5.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and add the\nsunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x-n+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(x\\le n-1\\) lie on a vertical line; the only point not yet\ncovered is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).  Hence a configuration with exactly\none sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 5.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\]\n\ncontain the six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Case \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The points that remain\nuncovered are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\\\\n&(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThey are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\frac{n}{2}+1\n      &&\\text{through }(n,1),(n-2,2),\\\\\nL_2&:\\;y=-2x+2n-1\n      &&\\text{through }(n-1,1),(n-2,3),\\\\\nL_3&:\\;y=x-n+3\n      &&\\text{through }(n-2,1),(n-1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the\nlines are distinct.  Together with the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines we obtain \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines, exactly three of which are sunny, covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 } .\n\\]\n\nAll other values are ruled out by the impossibility arguments of Sections 3 and 4,\nand the three values listed above are realised by the explicit constructions\nof Section 5.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
