ith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n> The remaining \\(d=n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, so by (1) they cover at most \\(C(d)\\) points.  Using the bound of §1 for the sunny lines we obtain  \n> \\[\n> C(d)+2k\\ge |T_n|\n> \\Longleftrightarrow\n> \\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2+2k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]  \n> Multiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying gives \\(k(3-k)\\ge0\\).  Hence the inequality fails for every \\(k\\ge4\\); consequently **no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).**”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- The inequality \\(C(d)+2k\\ge |T_n|\\) is derived from the (incorrect) assertion that each sunny line contributes at most **2** points. Since Section 1 is false, this inequality is **invalid**.  \n- Even if we accepted the (unproved) maximality of non‑sunny lines from Section 2, the bound on sunny lines is wrong, so the derived inequality \\(k(3-k)\\ge0\\) does **not** follow from the actual geometry.  \n- Consequently the conclusion “no configuration with \\(k\\ge4\\) exists” is **unsupported**.  \n\n**Result:** Critical Error – the impossibility claim rests on the false bound from Section 1, rendering the argument invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\n> “Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n> The two sunny lines can cover at most four points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the other \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \n> Consider the three points  \n> \\[\n> P_1=(n-2,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-2,2),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,1).\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> Any line joining two of them is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); hence none of the three lines can be sunny.  Consequently each of the points in (2) must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \n> A non‑sunny line can contain at most two of the points in (2):  \n> * a vertical line \\(x=n-2\\) contains \\(P_1,P_2\\);  \n> * a vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) contains \\(P_3\\);  \n> * a diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\) contains \\(P_2,P_3\\);  \n> * a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(P_1,P_3\\).  \n> Thus at least **two** of the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are forced to be \\(x=n-2\\) and \\(x=n-1\\) (or an equivalent pair).  After those two lines are used, every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(x\\ge n-2\\) is already covered **except** the three points listed in (2).  The two sunny lines can together cover at most four points, but the three points of (2) are pairwise non‑sunny, so at most one of them can be covered by a sunny line.  Hence at least one point of (2) stays uncovered – a contradiction.  \n> Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**”\n\n*Verification:*  \n1. **Incorrect use of the “at most four points” bound:** The claim that two sunny lines can cover at most four points again relies on the false statement from Section 1. Sunny lines can cover three points (e.g., slope 1 line through \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\)). Hence the “four‑point” bound is not guaranteed.  \n\n2. **Misidentification of uncovered points:**  \n   - The vertical line \\(x=n-2\\) (a non‑sunny line) **does** contain both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\).  \n   - The vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (a non‑sunny line) **does** contain \\(P_3\\).  \n   - Therefore after using those two vertical lines, the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) are already covered; they are **not** uncovered as the solution claims.  \n\n3. **Faulty deduction about forced lines:** The argument that “at least two of the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are forced to be \\(x=n-2\\) and \\(x=n-1\\)” is based on the (false) premise that the three points must be covered by non‑sunny lines *and* that they are not already covered. Since the premise is false, the deduction is unsound.  \n\n4. **Incorrect conclusion about remaining uncovered points:** Because the three points are already covered, the subsequent claim that “every point with \\(x\\ge n-2\\) is already covered except the three points listed” is false. In fact, after using the two vertical lines, the uncovered region consists of the ten points  \n   \\[\n   (n-3,1\\!-\\!4),\\;(n-2,1\\!-\\!3),\\;(n-1,1\\!-\\!2),\\;(n,1),\n   \\]  \n   not the three points.  \n\n5. **Overall logical collapse:** The entire impossibility proof for \\(k=2\\) rests on the erroneous identification of uncovered points and on the false “four‑point” bound. Consequently the conclusion that \\(k=2\\) is impossible is **unproved**.  \n\n**Result:** Critical Error – the argument contains multiple false statements and invalid deductions.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Constructions for the admissible values\n\n#### 5.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\n> “The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n*Verification:* For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). All lines are non‑sunny. **Correct.**\n\n#### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\n> “Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny) and add the sunny line \\(L:\\;y=x-n+1\\) (slope 1). All points with \\(x\\le n-1\\) lie on a vertical line; the only point not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).”\n\n*Verification:*  \n- For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\), the vertical line \\(x=a\\) covers it.  \n- The remaining points have \\(a=n\\); the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\). Indeed \\((n,1)\\) satisfies the line equation \\(1 = n - n + 1\\).  \n- The line \\(y=x-n+1\\) has slope 1, which is sunny.  \n- All lines are distinct. **Correct.**\n\n#### 5.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \n> “The three sunny lines \\(y=x,\\; y=-2x+5,\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) contain the six points of \\(T_3\\).”\n\n*Verification:* Direct substitution (as shown in the analysis) confirms each of the six points is on one of the three lines. Slopes \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\) are all sunny, and the lines are distinct. **Correct.**\n\n*Case \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \n> “Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The points that remain uncovered are  \n> \\[\n>